   <text:p text:style-name="P38">La perdita sarebbe molto più alta.</text:p>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_3" text:outline-level="3"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___Toc433_447025415"/>Perché si usa il logaritmo<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___Toc433_447025415"/></text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="P38">Il logaritmo penalizza <text:span text:style-name="T34">sensibilmente</text:span> le previsioni corrette a cui il modello assegna, <text:span text:style-name="T35">sbagliando,</text:span> probabilità basse. Con il logaritmo naturale:</text:p>
   <table:table table:name="TableProbabilitàPerdita" table:style-name="TableProbabilitàPerdita">
    <table:table-column table:style-name="TableProbabilitàPerdita.A"/>
    <table:table-column table:style-name="TableProbabilitàPerdita.B"/>
    <table:table-header-rows>
     <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="TableProbabilitàPerdita.A1" office:value-type="string">
       <text:p text:style-name="P42">Probabilità del token corretto</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="TableProbabilitàPerdita.B1" office:value-type="string">
       <text:p text:style-name="P42">Perdita</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
     </table:table-row>
    </table:table-header-rows>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="TableProbabilitàPerdita.A2" office:value-type="float" office:value="1">
      <text:p text:style-name="P43">1,000</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
